kground tools, permissions, a\n \n ## `scripts/run.sh`\n \n-the one runner. Holds the role:level → model/effort table, a git preflight (exits 2 outside a git work tree or before the first commit), the verifier family rule (a verifier never runs on the build's model family: a Fable or Sonnet build moves it to Opus at the same effort; the build is the newest builder result in the handoff dir or, when it holds none (a verifier launched at the task dir), the newest builder result of the highest-numbered `step-<n>/` under it that holds one, leaving out `step-<n>.superseded-<k>/` dirs; `run-selftest.sh` case `task dir`), the plan-reviewer family rule (a plan-reviewer never runs on the newest planner result's model family: an Opus planner moves the review to Fable at the same effort, a Fable or Sonnet planner keeps Opus), and a post-run check that the saved result passes `results.py validate --role <role>` (header, key section with an allowed first value, declared sections in declared order; on failure one stderr line `run.sh: <role> result <reason>; see <file>` and exit 3, with the result file and `runs.jsonl` line kept; a builder Status or verifier Verdict `inherited red: <tests>` is accepted, and a declared section out of order, such as a verifier `## Criteria` before `## Verdict`, exits 3 naming `comes before`, `run-selftest.sh`); the result-pick heading comes from `results.py key <role>`, and read-only enforcement (Edit/Write disallowed for every role except builder). Read-only does not block Bash, so the verifier's no-git-state-changes rule lives in its prompt. After the git preflight it reads the target repo's toplevel `.nvmrc`, matches it against `$NVM_DIR` (else `~/.nvm`) `versions/node` (exact, or the highest numeric prefix match), and prepends that `bin` to PATH, or leaves PATH unchanged with one stderr line for a missing version or an alias; it never sources nvm, and `SDLC_DRY_RUN=1` shows the resolution. run.sh sets `BASH_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS` and `BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS` to 3600000 for the role run unless already higher (fanout sets them too), so a long foreground command is not moved to the background by Claude Code, which no PreToolUse hook sees (T-0105). It logs the run as `--output-format stream-json --verbose` (the `.log` is one JSON event per line) and saves the last `result` event holding the role's required heading, else the last one whose origin is not `task-notification`, else the last one, because a background-task notification can end the session with its own result. run.sh exports `SDLC_TMP_WORKTREES=<toplevel>/.worktrees/tmp/<role>-<pid>` and removes every worktree under it on any exit, then runs `git worktree prune`. The role runs in the background and run.sh waits on it, so INT, TERM, or HUP reach the exit handler at once rather than after the role exits: it sends a still-running role TERM, then KILL after 2 s, and only then cleans up, well inside `run_lifecycle.py`'s 5 s grace before its SIGKILL (`run-selftest.sh` case `term cleanup`). A run SIGKILLed anyway leaves its prefix behind, so a real run also calls `scripts/reap-tmp-worktrees.sh <toplevel> <own prefix>` at the start and again at exit: it removes each dead-owner prefix under `.worktrees/tmp` (a `<role>-<pid>` dir whose pid names no live process, never the caller's own) with its registered worktrees, then prunes; it never deletes a branch and never fails (`run-selftest.sh` case `stale prefix`). At run end it compares a before and after snapshot of worktrees and branches, excluding this run's own prefix, and when a stray appeared (created from inside a script or program, not the Bash tool text the hook sees) it prints a stderr line quoting `stray worktree appeared during this run` and records `stray_worktrees` and `stray_branches` fields in the `runs.jsonl` line; this is report only, it does not change the run's exit code, and a dry run takes no snapshot. For builder and verifier it also exports `SDLC_BASE_SHA`, the build-start base: HEAD at the step's first builder run, kept in the step dir's `base-sha` file (rewritten when missing, not an ancestor of HEAD, or passed by a resync merge, which moves it to the newest first-parent merge); every other role gets it unset. Scope criteria compare with `git diff --name-only \"$SDLC_BASE_SHA\"` plus untracked files, so they hold whether or not the work is committed later. When a role edits run.sh while it runs (a builder rewriting the file in place), the running process is unaffected: the whole body after `set -euo pipefail` is one `{ ... }` group ending in `exit`, so bash parses it before executing any of it, keeps its behavior, and still saves its result (`run-selftest.sh` case `self-edit`). When the runner exits 0 without a result file, `run_lifecycle.py` (the lifecycle owner) records the run as failed with exit 1 and prints a `wrote no result` stderr line naming the expected result and the `.log` (`lifecycle-selftest.py` case `test_exit_zero_without_result_fails`). Before it launches a role (dry runs too, never in the `run_lifecycle.py` child), run.sh sums `messages.py count --receiver` for the role name and the backend name and, when the sum is above 0, prints one stderr line `run.sh: <n> new message(s) for <role>; read with messages.py`; a failed count adds 0, and the notice never prints a body or changes the exit code.\n+the one runner. Holds the role:level → model/effort table, a git preflight (exits 2 outside a git work tree or before the first commit), the verifier family rule (a verifier never runs on the build's model family: a Fable or Sonnet build moves it to Opus at the same effort; the build is the newest builder result in the handoff dir or, when it holds none (a verifier launched at the task dir), the newest builder result of the highest-numbered `step-<n>/` under it that holds one, leaving out `step-<n>.superseded-<k>/` dirs; `run-selftest.sh` case `task dir`), the plan-reviewer family rule (a plan-reviewer never runs on the newest planner result's mod