nd run.sh waits on it, so INT, TERM, or HUP reach the exit handler at once rather than after the role exits: it sends a still-running role TERM, then KILL after 2 s, and only then cleans up, well inside `run_lifecycle.py`'s 5 s grace before its SIGKILL (`run-selftest.sh` case `term cleanup`). A run SIGKILLed anyway leaves its prefix behind, so a real run also calls `scripts/reap-tmp-worktrees.sh <toplevel> <own prefix>` at the start and again at exit: it removes each dead-owner prefix under `.worktrees/tmp` (a `<role>-<pid>` dir whose pid names no live process, never the caller's own) with its registered worktrees, then prunes; it never deletes a branch and never fails (`run-selftest.sh` case `stale prefix`). At run end it compares a before and after snapshot of worktrees and branches, excluding this run's own prefix, and when a stray appeared (created from inside a script or program, not the Bash tool text the hook sees) it prints a stderr line quoting `stray worktree appeared during this run` and records `stray_worktrees` and `stray_branches` fields in the `runs.jsonl` line; this is report only, it does not change the run's exit code, and a dry run takes no snapshot. For builder and verifier it also exports `SDLC_BASE_SHA`, the build-start base: HEAD at the step's first builder run, kept in the step dir's `base-sha` file (rewritten when missing, not an ancestor of HEAD, or passed by a resync merge, which moves it to the newest first-parent merge); every other role gets it unset. Scope criteria compare with `git diff --name-only \"$SDLC_BASE_SHA\"` plus untracked files, so they hold whether or not the work is committed later. When a role edits run.sh while it runs (a builder rewriting the file in place), the running process is unaffected: the whole body after `set -euo pipefail` is one `{ ... }` group ending in `exit`, so bash parses it before executing any of it, keeps its behavior, and still saves its result (`run-selftest.sh` case `self-edit`). When the runner exits 0 without a result file, `run_lifecycle.py` (the lifecycle owner) records the run as failed with exit 1 and prints a `wrote no result` stderr line naming the expected result and the `.log` (`lifecycle-selftest.py` case `test_exit_zero_without_result_fails`). Before it launches a role (dry runs too, never in the `run_lifecycle.py` child), run.sh sums `messages.py count --receiver` for the role name and the backend name and, when the sum is above 0, prints one stderr line `run.sh: <n> new message(s) for <role>; read with messages.py`; a failed count adds 0, and the notice never prints a body or changes the exit code. Session limit (T-0260): when a run fails and its log shows a 429 or a session or usage limit, `run.sh` writes `<role>-<level>.limit` (one JSON line with the resume command and the matching log line) and prints `run.sh: session limit: resume with: <command>`; the exit code stays 1 and a later success removes the marker (`run-selftest.sh` case `session limit`, with a plain-failure control).\n \n ## `scripts/results.py`\n \n@@ -36,7 +36,7 @@ an agent's frontmatter `skills:` list is loaded by `run.sh` (appended with `--ap\n \n ## `scripts/tickets.py`\n \n-backs `/ticket`. `depends` also takes `file:<path>` (met once the repo-relative path exists, never orders) and `cap:<name>` (met once a `done` ticket lists it in `provides`, orders after every provider). `new <type> <slug> [\"title\"]` stamps a ticket with the next id, and `new` and `close` follow-ups take the next id above the max across the same-relative ticket root in every `git worktree list` tree (local root only outside git); `check` fails on two tickets sharing an id and names both folders, which also catches branches not checked out in any worktree after merge, `close <slug> [--force] [--rewrite-refs]` (run by `/file`; `close <slug> --reason \"<text>\"` closes a ticket finished by other work, without a filer file; `close <slug> --ack-warnings \"<why>\"` closes while Warnings are open, handles fo