ed] | roadmap [--numbered] | check [--numbered] | archive [--dry-run] [--rewrite-refs] [--force] [--numbered] | number [--dry-run]\"\n15:4. `close <slug> [--force]` is normally run by `/file` after the filer, not by hand. Each Follow-ups bullet starts with a tag: `fix-now:` (small related work, finished in this session), `decide:` (needs a decision or is out of scope), or `note:` (worth remembering, no action); an untagged bullet counts as `decide:`, and the title, slug, and duplicate check use the text after the tag. Close files at most one ticket: `--ticket N` makes item N, which must be a `decide:` item, a proposed idea ticket, and needs `--expect <digest>`; it refuses, writing nothing, on a `fix-now:` or `note:` item, a duplicate, an index out of range or in `--skip`, or with `--reason`. Without `--ticket`, close files no ticket. Every other item that is not skipped or a duplicate is appended to `.sdlc/notes.md` under `## <id>: <title> (<date>)` as `- <tag>: <text>`. `--reason \"<text>\"` closes a ticket finished by other work, skips the filer, files no follow-ups, and appends a `## Closed` section. `--ack-warnings \"<why>\"` closes while filer Warnings are open, handles follow-ups as a plain close does, and appends a `## Closed` section with the date, the reason, and the quoted warnings; it refuses with `--force` or `--reason`, with empty text, or when no warnings are open, and with `--dry-run` it passes the gate and writes nothing. `--dry-run` applies the same refusals, lists each follow-up as `<i>. <tag>: <title>` with a mark (`[<slug>] ticket` for the `--ticket` item, `note` for one bound for notes.md, `skip (--skip)`, or `duplicate of T-NNNN`), then one `open T-NNNN <title> [<slug>]` line per other proposed, ready, or blocked ticket, plus each open ticket that exists only in another git worktree's same-relative ticket root as `open T-NNNN <title> [<slug>] (worktree <name>)` after the local lines (a local copy decides any id it holds), and writes nothing, notes.md included (`--dry-run --ticket N` needs no `--expect`); it ends with an `expect <digest>` line over the full bullets, tags included. `--skip N[,N...]` drops the named indices (neither ticket nor notes.md), needs `--expect <digest>`, and refuses, writing nothing, when the Follow-ups list changed since that dry run. A follow-up that exactly duplicates another ticket (normalized title or slug) is skipped automatically; the ticket being closed never counts. `--reason` does not combine with `--dry-run`, `--skip`, `--expect`, or `--ticket`.\n18:- Numbered folders are opt-in, by `--numbered` on `new`, `close`, `roadmap`, or `check` for one call, or a `ticket_folders: numbered` line in `.sdlc/config` for the repo (a successful `new --numbered` or `close --numbered` appends that line when it is missing, so later plain calls stay numbered; plain `check` warns, without failing, when every folder has an `NNNN-` prefix but the config lacks the line); in numbered mode `new` names the folder `NNNN-<slug>` after the id it assigns (give the bare slug), `close` does the same for its one follow-up ticket, and `check` fails and `roadmap` refuses (writing nothing) on a `NNNN-` folder whose id is not `T-NNNN`; in numbered mode `close` also renames the folder to `.NNNN-<slug>` (first it runs `git grep --untracked -lF` from the git toplevel for the folder path `<rel>/NNNN-<slug>` (leaving out the ticket's own folder and `<root>/roadmap.md`) and refuses, writing nothing, naming each file that cites it. The scan finds the folder name, then keeps files with a literal `<rel>/<slug>` (counted only where it starts a path token, at the start of the text or after whitespace, a quote, a backtick, a paren, or a bracket, so `other/<rel>/<slug>`, an absolute path into another tree, and `../other/<rel>/<slug>` are not references, and neither is a URL or prefixed form such as `https://.../<rel>/<slug>` or `key=<rel>/<slug>`; never running on into a longer folder name) or a path token that resolves to the folder, whether toplevel-relative, relative to the citing file's directory, or absolute. Only matched tokens are rewritten, keeping their form; there is no case folding, symlink resolution, `~` expansion or self-reference handling. `--rewrite-refs` rewrites those references to `<rel>/.NNNN-<slug>` with the rename (`--dry-run --rewrite-refs` lists one `rewrite <file>` line each), and `--force` closes and leaves them (outside git there is no scan); hidden, still a closed ticket to every tool, in plain mode too, so archived tickets and their ids stay visible to `depends` and `new`), `check` fails on a dot folder that is not done or dropped, and `archive [--dry-run] [--rewrite-refs] [--force]` renames existing done/dropped numbered folders the same way, after running the same reference scan as `close` for every folder it would rename (a file inside another folder archived in the same run counts), and refuses, writing nothing, naming each citing file; `--rewrite-refs` rewrites them to the dot form before renaming (`--dry-run --rewrite-refs` lists one `rewrite <file>` line each), `--force` archives and leaves them, and outside git there is no scan; `_templates` and other dot entries stay skipped; without either, plain slugs, as before. To convert an existing plain-slug root, run `number [--dry-run]`: it renames each `<slug>` ticket folder to `NNNN-<slug>` after its id, rewrites every cite of `<rel>/<slug>` (the same scan, plus files inside the folder) to `<rel>/NNNN-<slug>`, persists the config line, and regenerates the roadmap, refusing and writing nothing on a bad ticket, a non `T-NNNN` id, or an existing target; follow with `archive --rewrite-refs` to hide done and dropped folders.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S1dL2b3yYSqNgz1nYGdmnu","type":"tool_result","content":"=== docs mentions of each fix ===\nugrep: error: error at position 177\n(?:[^\\n\\x80-\\xbf][\\x80-\\xbf]*){0,120}\n        exceeds complexity limits___/\n\n\n=== .limit glob risk ===\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:240:    t = max(tasks, key=lambda d: max((p.stat().st_mtime for p in d.rglob('*')), default=0))\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:254:    if any(any(d.glob('builder-L5.*')) for d in live_step_dirs(t)):\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:684:        before = sorted(p for p in clean.rglob('*'))\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/run_lifecycle.py:83:        while any(directory.glob(name + '.*')):\n=== .limit readers ===\ndocs/maintenance/sdlc-internals.md:11:the one runner. Holds the role:level → model/effort table, a git preflight (exits 2 outside a git work tree or before the first commit), the verifier family rule (a verifier never runs on the build's model family: a Fable or Sonnet build moves it to Opus at the same effort; the build is the newest builder result in the handoff dir or, when it holds none (a verifier launched at the task dir), the newest builder result of the highest-numbered `step-<n>/` under it that holds one, leaving out `step-<n>.superseded-<k>/` dirs; `run-selftest.sh` case `task dir`), the plan-reviewer family rule (a plan-reviewer never runs on the newest planner result's model family: an Opus planner moves the review to Fable at the same effort, a Fable or Sonnet planner keeps Opus), and a post-run check that the saved result passes `results.py validate --role <role>` (header, key section with an allowed first value, declared sections in declared order; on failure one stderr line `run.sh: <role> result <reason>; see <file>` and exit 3, with the result file and `runs.jsonl` line kept; a builder Status or verifier Verdict `inherited red: <tests>` is accepted, and a declared section out of order, such as a verifier `## Criteria` before `## Verdict`, exits 3 naming `comes before`, `run-selftest.sh`); the result-pick heading comes from `results.py key <role>`, and read-only enforcement (Edit/Write disallowed for every role except builder). Read-only does not block Bash, so the verifier's no-git-state-changes rule lives in its prompt. After the git preflight it reads the target repo's toplevel `.nvmrc`, matches it against `$NVM_DIR` (else `~/.nvm`) `versions/node` (exact, or the highest numeric prefix match), and prepends that `bin` to PATH, or leaves PATH unchanged with one stderr line for a missing version or an alias; it never sources nvm, and `SDLC_DRY_RUN=1` shows the resolution. run.sh sets `BASH_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS` and `BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS` to 3600000 for the role run unless already higher (fanout sets them too), so a long foreground command is not moved to the background by Claude Code, which no PreToolUse hook sees (T-0105). It logs the run as `--output-format stream-json --verbose` (the `.log` is one JSON event per line) and saves the last `result` event holding the role's required heading, else the last one whose origin is not `task-notification`, else the last one, because a background-task notification can end the session with its own result. run.sh exports `SDLC_TMP_WORKTREES=<toplevel>/.worktrees/tmp/<role>-<pid>` and removes every worktree under it on any exit, then runs `git worktree prune`. The role runs in the background and run.sh waits on it, so INT, TERM, or HUP reach the exit handler at once rather than after the role exits: it sends a still-running role TERM, then KILL after 2 s, and only then cleans up, well inside `run_lifecycle.py`'s 5 s grace before its SIGKILL (`run-selftest.sh` case `term cleanup`). A run SIGKILLed anyway leaves its prefix behind, so a real run also calls `scripts/reap-tmp-worktrees.sh <toplevel> <own prefix>` at the start and again at exit: it removes each dead-owner prefix under `.worktrees/tmp` (a `<role>-<pid>` dir whose pid names no live process, never the caller's own) with its registered worktrees, then prunes; it never deletes a branch and never fails (`run-selftest.sh` case `stale prefix`). At run end it compares a before and after snapshot of worktrees and branches, excluding this run's own prefix, and when a stray appeared (created from inside a script or program, not the Bash tool text the hook sees) it prints a stderr line quoting `stray worktree appeared during this run` and records `stray_worktrees` and `stray_branches` fields in the `runs.jsonl` line; this is report only, it does not change the run's exit code, and a dry run takes no snapshot. For builder and verifier it also exports `SDLC_BASE_SHA`, the build-start base: HEAD at the step's first builder run, kept in the step dir's `base-sha` file (rewritten when missing, not an ancestor of HEAD, or passed by a resync merge, which moves it to the newest first-parent merge); every other role gets it unset. Scope criteria compare with `git diff --name-only \"$SDLC_BASE_SHA\"` plus untracked files, so they hold whether or not the work is committed later. When a role edits run.sh while it runs (a builder rewriting the file in place), the running process is unaffected: the whole body after `set -euo pipefail` is one `{ ... }` group ending in `exit`, so bash parses it before executing any of it, keeps its behavior, and still saves its result (`run-selftest.sh` case `self-edit`). When the runner exits 0 without a result file, `run_lifecycle.py` (the lifecycle owner) records the run as failed with exit 1 and prints a `wrote no result` stderr line naming the expected result and the `.log` (`lifecycle-selftest.py` case `test_exit_zero_without_result_fails`). Before it launches a role (dry runs too, never in the `run_lifecycle.py` child), run.sh sums `messages.py count --receiver` for the role name and the backend name and, when the sum is above 0, prints one stderr line `run.sh: <n> new message(s) for <role>; read with messages.py`; a failed count adds 0, and the notice never prints a body or changes the exit code. Session limit (T-0260): when a run fails and its log shows a 429 or a session or usage limit, `run.sh` writes `<role>-<level>.limit` (one JSON line with the resume command and the matching log line) and prints `run.sh: session limit: resume with: <command>`; the exit code stays 1 and a later success removes the marker (`run-selftest.sh` case `session limit`, with a plain-failure control).\ndocs/maintenance/sdlc-internals.md:100:Starts the solo agent for one ticket and records its cost (T-0225): `solo_run.py run --task-dir <ticket folder> --tier S|M|L --brief <text>` (also `--brief-file`, `--backend claude|codex`, `--cwd`, `--timeout`). It starts `claude -p --agent sdlc-lite:solo --output-format json` at the tier's model and effort (the same table as `fanout.SOLO_TIERS` and `agents/solo.md`, guarded by `check.py` item 27), or `codex_exec.py solo L1..L3`, prints the report, and appends one `role solo` line to `<task-dir>/runs.jsonl` in the run.sh record shape (ts, level L1..L3, model, effort, cost_usd, usage, duration_ms, turns, attempt_id, backend, transport, routing_reason, exit_code). `budget.py` and the board count it because they sum `cost_usd` over every `runs.jsonl` under the ticket. Cost is never guessed: a crash, a missing result event, a result without `total_cost_usd`, and Codex (which reports none) record `cost_usd` null with a `cost_reason`, and the budget reports that run as unmetered. The raw command in `agents/solo.md` still works and records nothing. Fanout's solo driver starts through `solo_run.py` too (T-0226), so each solo lane writes its own `role solo` record in the ticket's `runs.jsonl` and the budget and board meter the parallel default path; a Codex lane records a null cost, counted as unmetered. `SDLC_SELFTEST_RAW_SOLO` restores the raw command for the fanout selftest's negative control (case 17). Budget gate (T-0229): before it starts anything, `solo_run.py` runs the same check `run.sh` does for roles (`budget.report` on the ticket holding `--task-dir`): at 80 percent of `budget_usd` or more with no `budget.py ack` it prints `solo_run.py: budget <pct>%: ask the user; budget.py ack` and exits 4 with no session and no record; a ticket with no budget, or a task folder with no ticket id, is never blocked. Fanout's solo lanes go through it, so a gated lane stops as `driver failed rc=4 (budget gate: budget.py ack)` (fanout selftest case 18). `--new-task [slug]` (for `/agent solo` with no named ticket) creates `<ticket root>/<slug>/handoff.md` from the brief, with no `id` and so no budget, and runs there, so that run still leaves a `role solo` record. Metering covers Claude solo lanes only: Codex records cost null, and a `full` fanout lane or a conductor session writes no record. `solo_run.py selftest` runs against a stub `claude` on PATH. Session limit (T-0260): a failed session whose output shows a 429 or a session or usage limit writes `<task dir>/solo.limit` (resume command, evidence) and prints a resume hint on stderr; fanout WIP-commits the marker with the lane, and a later success removes it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d37f6391-7843-414c-9b5f-3ff80d3a3d36","uuid":"a5f3bae5-90b2-4b2f-8833-f85eea8ac20b","timestamp":"2026-10-05T23:33:28.423Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== docs mentions of each fix ===\nugrep: error: error at position 177\n(?:[^\\n\\x80-\\xbf][\\x80-\\xbf]*){0,120}\n        exceeds complexity limits___/\n\n\n=== .limit glob risk ===\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:240:    t = max(tasks, key=lambda d: max((p.stat().st_mtime for p in d.rglob('*')), default=0))\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:254:    if any(any(d.glob('builder-L5.*')) for d in live_step_dirs(t)):\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/board.py:684:        before = sorted(p for p in clean.rglob('*'))\nplugins/sdlc-lite/scripts/run_lifecycle.py:83:        while any(directory.glob(name + '.*')):\n=== .limit r